{"event":"trap","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-2/branch-0","trapId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-2/branch-0/trap-0","ordinal":0,"type":"job","arg":{"spec":"judge","input":{"item":{"text":"# AI Research Briefing — Monday, November 16, 2026\n\n**TEST EDITION — produced by the AgentTS briefing workflow (BRA-55 proof run), not the live 06:15 cron.** Covers arXiv submissions 2026-11-09–2026-11-16 in cs.LG, cs.CL, cs.CV, cs.AI, stat.ML, cs.NE, deduped against a copy of the seen list (2892 papers scanned, 50 seen IDs excluded). All 7 papers below were verified on their own arXiv abs pages, with full-text reads where ar5iv served the paper; every number and claim is an extracted sentence from the fetched text. The must-read is 2611.18482.\n\n---\n\n## 1. MMFlash: Faster Speculative Decoding for Vision-Language Models\n\n**arXiv: [2611.18482](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18482)** · Sicheng Feng , Zigeng Chen , Song Wang , Gongfan Fang , Xinchao Wang · Submitted 13 Nov 2026\n\n**Why it matters:** Speculative decoding accelerates generation by letting a lightweight drafter propose tokens for parallel verification by a target model. Diffusion drafters such as DFlash propose an entire token block in a single forward pass, but mainly focus on text-only tasks, leaving multimodal drafting underexplored.\n\n### Key findings\n\n- We find that acceptance rates decrease from 46% in the lowest visual-dependence quartile to 22% in the highest, with this disparity persisting after multimodal training.\n- Across various multimodal benchmarks, MMFlash achieves lossless decoding speedups of up to 3.58, outperforming EAGLE-3 and DFlash by up to 195.4% and 17.1%, respectively.\n\n### How it works\n\n- Motivated by this observation, we propose MMFlash, a simple and effective framework that adaptively upweights visually dependent tokens that the drafter predicts poorly.\n\n### How it relates to other work\n\n- Shares block, verification, diffusion, speculative, decoding with “BV Loss: Block Verification-Aware Loss for Block Diffusion Speculative Decoding” (2026-09-29, arXiv:2609.34832).\n- Shares multimodal, drafting, speculative, decoding with “HAWK: Rethinking Multimodal Drafting for Speculative Decoding” (2026-10-04, arXiv:2610.00623).\n- Shares diffusion, drafting, speculative, decoding with “D-Loop: Looped Diffusion Drafting for Speculative Decoding” (2026-10-08, arXiv:2610.06011).\n- Pairs with #2 (2611.18057): both papers turn on attention, propose — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n- Pairs with #3 (2611.08669): both papers turn on speculative, decoding, drafter, parallel — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n\n### Insights beyond the paper\n\n- Read with the companion papers in this edition — the shared-term links above name the concrete overlap; check whether it is a shared method or just shared vocabulary.\n\n### Honest limitations\n\n- No limitation sentences were extracted from the fetched paper text; the claims above are the authors' own and unchallenged here.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SWAM: Saliency-Weighted Asymmetric Merging for Efficient Vision Transformers\n\n**arXiv: [2611.18057](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18057)** · Utkarsh Kumar Singh , Manish Das , Shiv Ram Dubey , Satish Kumar Singh · Submitted 13 Nov 2026\n\n**Why it matters:** Vision Transformers (ViTs) achieve strong performance on image recognition tasks, yet their quadratic self-attention complexity over input tokens creates prohibitive computational costs for practical deployment. Token reduction methods offer a practical solution by progressively reducing the token sequence during the forward pass.\n\n### Key findings\n\n- A lightweight MLP-based saliency prediction module (75k parameters per stage, 1% of backbone parameters) supervised via attention rollout is trained end-to-end jointly with a DeiT-Small backbone using a composite cross-entropy, knowledge-distillation, and saliency-alignment objective.\n- On CIFAR-100, TinyImageNet, and ImageNet-100, under a conservative schedule that reduces tokens from 196 to 100 patch tokens, with the CLS token retained throughout, 27.5% FLOPs reduction and 1.35 times speedup, SWAM matches or exceeds baseline across the reported results.\n- Under an aggressive schedule (196 to 70 tokens with the CLS token kept throughout, 33.8% FLOPs reduction, and 1.46 times speedup), accuracy gains are achieved on ImageNet-100 (+0.23%) with minimal losses on CIFAR-100 and TinyImageNet.\n\n### How it works\n\n- We propose Saliency-Weighted Asymmetric Merging (SWAM), a training-based token merging framework that extends the Bipartite Soft Matching (BSM) with two saliency-aware mechanisms.\n- We introduce a two-step matching formulation in which cosine similarity decides candidate pairs, while a multiplicative maximum saliency penalty regulates their selection for merging.\n\n### How it relates to other work\n\n- Shares quadratic, attention, vision, transformers with “Beyond Quadratic Attention: Separable Gaussian Operators for ODE Vision Transformers (SSoG)” (2026-11-06, arXiv:2611.08236).\n- Shares alignment, vision, transformers with “Align Once to Explain: Feature Alignment for Scalable B-cosification of Foundational Vision Transformers” (2026-11-04, arXiv:2611.03438).\n- Shares attention, transformers with “From Attention Sensitivity to Layer Role: Revisiting Mixed-Precision Quantization of Transformers” (2026-09-29, arXiv:2609.34866).\n- Pairs with #1 (2611.18482): both papers turn on propose, attention — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n- Pairs with #4 (2611.16345): both papers turn on attention, quadratic, speedup, accuracy — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n\n### Insights beyond the paper\n\n- Read with the companion papers in this edition — the shared-term links above name the concrete overlap; check whether it is a shared method or just shared vocabulary.\n\n### Honest limitations\n\n- No limitation sentences were extracted from the fetched paper text; the claims above are the authors' own and unchallenged here.\n\n---\n\n## 3. ChainFlow: Few-Step Latent Flow Matching for Speculative Decoding\n\n**arXiv: [2611.08669](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08669)** · Selim Aktas , Yusuf Erdem Erdem , Ahmed Zeer , Himmet Toprak Kesgin , Muzaffer Kaan Yuce , Mehmet Fatih Amasyali · Submitted 5 Nov 2026\n\n**Why it matters:** Speculative decoding has become a standard way to accelerate LLM inference: a lightweight drafter proposes tokens that the target verifies in one parallel pass. Existing drafters face trade-offs that limits their speedups: autoregressive drafters generate tokens one at a time, so drafting cost grows with draft length, while diffusion drafters generate tokens in parallel but predict each position independently, ignoring inter-token dependencies.\n\n### Key findings\n\n- On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively.\n\n### How it works\n\n- We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states.\n\n### How it relates to other work\n\n- Shares speculative, decoding, cost, parallel, drafter with “LongSpark: Efficient speculative decoding with a fixed-cost parallel drafter” (2026-10-02, arXiv:2609.37029).\n- Shares diffusion, drafting, speculative, decoding with “D-Loop: Looped Diffusion Drafting for Speculative Decoding” (2026-10-08, arXiv:2610.06011).\n- Shares speculative, decoding, diffusion, drafting with “One Call Ahead: Packed Self-Speculative Decoding with Diffusion Drafting” (2026-10-31, arXiv:2610.33379).\n- Pairs with #1 (2611.18482): both papers turn on speculative, decoding, drafter, parallel — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n- Pairs with #4 (2611.16345): both papers turn on diffusion, generate, denoising, autoregressive — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n\n### Insights beyond the paper\n\n- Read with the companion papers in this edition — the shared-term links above name the concrete overlap; check whether it is a shared method or just shared vocabulary.\n\n### Honest limitations\n\n- No limitation sentences were extracted from the fetched paper text; the claims above are the authors' own and unchallenged here.\n\n---\n\n## 4. DRAMA: Dissecting Attention Redundancy for Accelerating Multimodal Diffusion Large Language Models\n\n**arXiv: [2611.16345](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16345)** · Yi-Hsin Hung , Fangfu Liu , Shao-Yuan Lo , Chu-Song Chen · Submitted 12 Nov 2026\n\n**Why it matters:** Multimodal diffusion large language models (dLLMs) generate text through iterative masked denoising with bidirectional attention, offering a compelling alternative to autoregressive vision-language modeling. However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost.\n\n### Key findings\n\n- Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy.\n\n### How it works\n\n- The fetched text yielded no method sentences matching the extraction patterns; see the abstract above.\n\n### How it relates to other work\n\n- Shares dllm, adaptive, sparse, attention, diffusion, inference with “Step-dLLM: Adaptive Step-aware Sparse Attention for Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference” (2026-11-04, arXiv:2611.01320).\n- Shares autoregressive, masked, diffusion, inference with “Zarya: A Hybrid Autoregressive–Masked Diffusion Language Model with Flexible Training and Dual-Mode Inference” (2026-10-01, arXiv:2609.19868).\n- Shares denoising, diffusion, modeling with “Denoising Hierarchical Representations: Joint Continuous Diffusion for Language Modeling” (2026-10-07, arXiv:2610.08738).\n- Pairs with #1 (2611.18482): both papers turn on diffusion, multimodal, distributions, attention — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n- Pairs with #2 (2611.18057): both papers turn on quadratic, attention, speedup, baseline — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n\n### Insights beyond the paper\n\n- Context from the paper's own text: However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost.\n- Read with the companion papers in this edition — the shared-term links above name the concrete overlap; check whether it is a shared method or just shared vocabulary.\n\n### Honest limitations\n\n- However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Beyond Similarity Entropy: A Rank-Guided Data-Free Quantization Framework for Vision Transformers\n\n**arXiv: [2611.11320](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11320)** · Runqing Jiang , Ye Zhang , Pengpeng Yu , Zhuo Song , Changjun Li , Longguang Wang , Yulan Guo · Submitted 8 Nov 2026\n\n**Why it matters:** Vision Transformers (ViTs) have demonstrated remarkable performance, yet their computational and memory demands pose significant challenges for deployment on mobile devices. Data-free quantization (DFQ) enables the compression of ViTs without access to their original training data.\n\n### Key findings\n\n- No number-bearing sentences were extracted from the fetched text; the abstract's claims are quoted in “Why it matters” above.\n\n### How it works\n\n- To address this limitation, we propose RankQ-ViT, a rank-guided DFQ framework for ViTs.\n- Motivated by this observation, we introduce a block-aware self-contrastive objective that promotes feature diversity during synthetic data generation without explicitly computing matrix rank at each optimization step.\n\n### How it relates to other work\n\n- Shares reconstruction, optimization, quantization with “The Devil Is in the Reconstruction Loss Scale: Rethinking Optimization in LLM Quantization” (2026-10-02, arXiv:2610.00983).\n- Shares feature, compression, reconstruction, free with “SlimKV: Joint Token-Feature KV Cache Compression with Reconstruction-Free Beacon Attention” (2026-10-05, arXiv:2610.02953).\n- Shares block, multiple, vision, transformers with “One Block, Multiple Depths: Recurrent Vision Transformers with Depth-Programmed Experts (reViT)” (2026-10-11, arXiv:2610.12448).\n- Pairs with #1 (2611.18482): both papers turn on propose, motivated, observation — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n- Pairs with #2 (2611.18057): both papers turn on transformers, propose, entropy, objective — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n\n### Insights beyond the paper\n\n- Context from the paper's own text: However, similarity entropy describes the dispersion of similarity values without ensuring that the generated samples cover diverse feature directions, potentially limiting their effectiveness for quantization calibration.\n- Read with the companion papers in this edition — the shared-term links above name the concrete overlap; check whether it is a shared method or just shared vocabulary.\n\n### Honest limitations\n\n- However, similarity entropy describes the dispersion of similarity values without ensuring that the generated samples cover diverse feature directions, potentially limiting their effectiveness for quantization calibration.\n- To address this limitation, we propose RankQ-ViT, a rank-guided DFQ framework for ViTs.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Joint Quantization and Dynamic Partitioning for Context-Aware Edge Vision Transformers\n\n**arXiv: [2611.14196](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14196)** · Zhu Jiaqi , Jain Prakriti , Katare Dewant , Kumar Saurabh · Submitted 10 Nov 2026\n\n**Why it matters:** Deploying vision transformer models across connected and automated vehicles (CAVs) requires joint management of computation and Vehicle-to-Vehicle (V2V) communication. Static split-inference methods use fixed partition points and cannot adapt to time-varying link bandwidth, processing load, and concurrent multi-stream traffic.\n\n### Key findings\n\n- Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by 33.3–36.0% relative to a Uniform INT8 split.\n- At K=8, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by 34.0% on the Orin Nano and 33.1% on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to 3.1–4.1 percentage points in mAP_50 relative to FP16 execution.\n\n### How it works\n\n- This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes.\n\n### How it relates to other work\n\n- Shares layer, mixed, precision, quantization, transformers with “From Attention Sensitivity to Layer Role: Revisiting Mixed-Precision Quantization of Transformers” (2026-09-29, arXiv:2609.34866).\n- Shares mixed, precision, quantization, inference with “PN-Mix: Pair-Norm Guided Mixed-Precision Key Cache Quantization for Efficient Long-Output LLM Inference” (2026-10-17, arXiv:2610.19605).\n- Shares mixed, precision, quantization, multi with “CoMixKV: Coordinated Mixed-Precision KV Cache Quantization for Efficient LLM-based Multi-agent Systems” (2026-11-15, arXiv:2611.10096).\n- Pairs with #2 (2611.18057): both papers turn on transformers, formulation, jointly, reduces — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n- Pairs with #3 (2611.08669): both papers turn on inference, concurrent — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n\n### Insights beyond the paper\n\n- Read with the companion papers in this edition — the shared-term links above name the concrete overlap; check whether it is a shared method or just shared vocabulary.\n\n### Honest limitations\n\n- No limitation sentences were extracted from the fetched paper text; the claims above are the authors' own and unchallenged here.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Token2Tile: Token-Wise Sparse Attention with Tile Execution through a Low-Rank Space for Video DiTs\n\n**arXiv: [2611.17930](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17930)** · Xuecheng Wu , Aichen Feng , Jinyan Chen , Qingjie Zhao , Julien Lai · Submitted 13 Nov 2026\n\n**Why it matters:** Attention is essential for modeling spatio-temporal dependencies in video diffusion transformers, but its quadratic cost makes it a major inference bottleneck. Its high sparsity offers an opportunity to accelerate generation by focusing computation on important interactions.\n\n### Key findings\n\n- On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense.\n- At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster.\n\n### How it works\n\n- We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution.\n\n### How it relates to other work\n\n- Shares sparse, dense, attention, diffusion, transformers with “MC-Sparse: Deconstructing and Closing the Dense–Sparse Attention Gap in Diffusion Transformers — arXiv:2610.06801” (2026-10-15, arXiv:2610.06801).\n- Shares attention, tiles, video, diffusion, transformers with “Triage: Skip, Skim, or Refine Attention Tiles in Video Diffusion Transformers” (2026-10-27, arXiv:2610.35706).\n- Shares sparse, attention, diffusion, inference with “Step-dLLM: Adaptive Step-aware Sparse Attention for Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference” (2026-11-04, arXiv:2611.01320).\n- Pairs with #1 (2611.18482): both papers turn on diffusion, attention — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n- Pairs with #2 (2611.18057): both papers turn on transformers, quadratic, attention, speedup — compare their numbers directly before trusting either.\n\n### Insights beyond the paper\n\n- Context from the paper's own text: However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles.\n- Read with the companion papers in this edition — the shared-term links above name the concrete overlap; check whether it is a shared method or just shared vocabulary.\n\n### Honest limitations\n\n- However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles.\n\n---\n\n**Must-read if you're short on time: MMFlash: Faster Speculative Decoding for Vision-Language Models (2611.18482)** — Speculative decoding accelerates generation by letting a lightweight drafter propose tokens for parallel verification by a target model.","goal":"AI research briefing test edition 2026-11-16"},"rubric":{"criteria":["concrete numbers","how it works","other work","limitations","insights"],"threshold":0.8},"judge":0}}}
